<%# A DATED BILL NOBODY HAS WRITTEN A RULE FOR. Local: `suggestion`.

    §8's copy target: `$600 in Mar and Sep → every 6 months, next Mar 1`. The lead is the BILL —
    what leaves the account, on what schedule, next when — and the per-period cost is the second
    line, because that is the figure the panel is ORDERED by and the one the structural check will
    add this rule to.

    THE GUESS IS SAID IN THE SENTENCE, not marked with a badge. A single-occurrence item is
    proposed with an interval nothing measured (§8's own rule), and three of the demo's ten bills
    are one-off purchases wearing a guessed annual interval — if a guessed row read the same as a
    measured one, a third of the panel would be noise the user could not tell from evidence. %>
<p>
  <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= suggestion.subject.name %></span> —
  <span class="tabular-nums"><%= number_to_currency(suggestion.amount) %></span>
  <%= suggestion_interval_label(suggestion.detail[:interval_months]) %>,
  next due <%= suggestion.detail[:due_on].strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %>.
</p>

<p class="text-gray-500">
  <% if suggestion.detail[:guessed] %>
    Seen once, on <%= suggestion.detail[:last_seen_on].strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %> — one payment is not a schedule,
    so <%= suggestion_interval_label(suggestion.detail[:interval_months]) %> is a guess.
    That makes <%= number_to_currency(suggestion.detail[:per_period_cost]) %> a period a guess too.
  <% else %>
    Paid <%= pluralize(suggestion.detail[:occurrences], "time") %>, last on
    <%= suggestion.detail[:last_seen_on].strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %>.
    Costs <%= number_to_currency(suggestion.detail[:per_period_cost]) %> a period.
  <% end %>
</p>
